Part 1. Facts survey

### 1.1. Facts given in the task
- Review metadata:
  - Title: Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for opportunistic infections in children with HIV infection
  - Objective: To assess the effects of routinely administered cotrimoxazole on death and illness episodes in children with HIV infection, and in infants of HIV-infected mothers.
  - Background:
    - Most HIV-infected children are in low-income countries without access to antiretroviral drugs.
    - Prevention and early treatment of opportunistic infections are key.
    - Cotrimoxazole (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) is inexpensive, broad-spectrum, effective against Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia (PCP), and generally safe.
    - HIV diagnosis in children is complicated by maternal antibodies; prophylaxis based on maternal status is a possible approach but challenging in low-resource settings and may increase drug resistance.
  - Selection criteria: Randomised or quasi-randomised trials comparing routine cotrimoxazole versus placebo or no treatment in:
    - Children <15 years with HIV infection; or
    - Children <18 months with HIV-infected mothers.

- Provided study list (study_index, title, abstract):
  - Study 1: CHAP trial, Zambia
    - Design: Double-blind randomized placebo-controlled trial in children aged 1–14 years with clinical features of HIV infection.
    - Primary outcomes: Mortality and adverse events possibly related to treatment.
    - Sample: 541 randomized; 7 later identified as HIV-negative and excluded (net analyzed N=534). Arm sizes not stated in the abstract.
    - Follow-up: Median 19 months; trial stopped early on DSMC advice (October 2003).
    - Mortality: 74 (28%) in cotrimoxazole vs 112 (42%) in placebo; HR 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77), p=0.0002.
